Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) - provides virtualized computing resources over the internet, such as storage, networking, and computing power. Examples include Amazon Web Services (AWS), Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud Platform.
Platform as a Service (PaaS) - provides a platform for developers to create, run, and manage their applications without the need to manage the underlying infrastructure. Examples include Heroku, Google App Engine, and Microsoft Azure.
Software as a Service (SaaS) - delivers software applications over the internet, allowing users to access them from any device with an internet connection. Examples include Salesforce, Dropbox, and Google Workspace (formerly G Suite).

Here are 4 storage services in Azure:
Azure Blob Storage: This is a massively scalable object storage for unstructured data like documents, images, videos, audio, and backups.
Azure Files: This is a fully managed file share in the cloud that can be accessed via Server Message Block (SMB) or Network File System (NFS) protocols.
Azure Queue Storage: This is a messaging service used to store and retrieve messages between applications.
Azure Disk Storage: This is a durable and high-performing managed disks solution for Virtual Machines and other Azure services that require block storage.

In Azure, NSG stands for Network Security Group, which is a fundamental component of the Azure networking model. It acts as a virtual firewall for controlling inbound and outbound network traffic to resources in an Azure Virtual Network. NSGs can be associated with either a subnet or a network interface, and they contain a set of security rules that define how network traffic is allowed or denied.
NSGs provide a powerful way to filter network traffic and enforce network security policies, such as restricting access to specific ports or IP addresses. They can be used to control access to Azure Virtual Machines, Azure SQL Databases, and other Azure resources. NSGs are also commonly used in conjunction with Azure Application Gateway and Azure Firewall for advanced networking scenarios.
In summary, NSG is a critical security feature in Azure that provides granular control over network traffic and helps to protect resources from unauthorized access.

Azure Resource Manager (ARM) is the deployment and management service for Azure. It provides a consistent management layer that enables you to create, update, and delete resources in your Azure subscription. With ARM, you can organize your resources in resource groups, apply access control to resources using role-based access control (RBAC), and deploy templates that consist of multiple resource types as one solution.
ARM provides a powerful set of APIs that you can use to manage your resources programmatically. You can also use Azure Portal, Azure CLI, Azure PowerShell, and Azure Cloud Shell to manage your resources using ARM. ARM makes it easy to manage resources at scale, allowing you to automate the deployment, management, and monitoring of your resources in a consistent and reliable way.

Microsoft Defender is an advanced threat protection solution offered by Microsoft that provides endpoint security for Windows-based devices. It uses artificial intelligence and machine learning technologies to detect and prevent attacks on devices, including malware, ransomware, and other types of malicious software.
Azure Security Center is a cloud security management service offered by Microsoft that provides unified security management for hybrid cloud workloads. It offers a centralized dashboard for monitoring security alerts and provides recommendations for improving security posture. It also integrates with Microsoft Defender to provide endpoint security for Azure-based resources. Azure Security Center provides security assessment and recommendations, threat protection, and posture management for resources deployed in Azure as well as resources running on-premises and in other clouds.
